-**The theory in one paragraph.** Nothing that exists is exhausted by what it has already become. Every state carries 間 (jiān — an unexhausted gap, the place where a state is not used up by what it already is), and existence is nothing but the unbroken taking-up of such gaps: 续(态) = 态 ⊕ 間(态). So 生生不息 does not mean that any particular form lasts forever, and it does not mean that complexity, options, growth or efficiency must keep rising; it means that the source of further generation is not exhausted and the way of transformation is not sealed — 不绝其源，不塞其流，不毁其后. A state is 开 (open) when four things hold at once: an unexhausted domain remains (未), becoming is still reachable (可), it can still respond (应), and it can still revise and recover (转). The loss of that is 闭, and closure comes in kinds that must never be conflated: 成闭, closure that does not reopen, is the evil; 护闭, closing here so that a wider field stays open, is a use of the mean; 元闭, freezing the rules that generate what counts as a legal move at all, is the deepest; and 伪开 — the appearance of openness that in fact produces closure — is the characteristic form of modern evil. Good is therefore not the maximisation of anything: **善不在于最大化什么，善在于不闭何处**; and at the level of an act it is 义 ∧ 正筛 — choosing rightly at this threshold *and* shifting the selection field so that open-protecting acts become likelier and capture-type acts harder to sustain. Because anyone who can ask "what ought I to do?" already exists, and existing already depends on the process continuing, no agent can coherently repudiate the process it is a form of; the further step — that one ought to keep those conditions open *for others too* — is not squeezed out of that fact but openly declared as a minimal public commitment, defended by what is destroyed when it is denied. From this it follows that alignment with 生生不息, rather than with human preference, is the only alignment target that can be grounded at all; that alignment is a property of 